// Between Frontiers: armed forces in peace (phase 5, slice A). Contract:
// crates/bf-sim/src/war/state.rs (PeaceRules); reasoning in docs/war.pt-BR.md, "Forças em paz".
// Licence: CC BY-SA 4.0.
//
// Owner's decisions 1 and 2 (2026-11-17), kept swappable here so that a "no" costs little:
// - `soldiers: Forces` puts in the pops the men of the formations of
//   data/scenario/1925/forces.ron; `PopulationShare(n)` goes back to the old rule, n per mille
//   of the population as soldiers and the state's fixed basket of arms.
// - `peace_size: ShareOfServiceAge` keeps each country's men under arms at the share of its men
//   of service age it had in 1925, scaled by the `conscription` effect of the law in force over
//   the one of 1925 when `law_scaling` is on; `Fixed1925` keeps the 1925 numbers.
// - `share_override`: a country's share of its men of service age under arms, per mille, in
//   place of the one measured in 1925.
#![enable(implicit_some)]
(
    // The rule while the forces are off (`forces` in data/rules.ron, off until the PR that
    // switches them on, judged by the aggregates on its own; `BF_RULES=forces` or
    // `bf-bench --rule forces` turn them on for a run, `GameData::enable_forces` in tests).
    soldiers: PopulationShare(10),
    peace_size: ShareOfServiceAge,
    law_scaling: true,
    // Weeks to make good the equipment a formation lacks (design parameter).
    replace_weeks: 26,
    // Years for a force to move to a newer generation of equipment once the country makes it
    // (Industrial stage at home, or the technology diffused somewhere: bought abroad). Design
    // parameter: armies re-equip over a decade or so, unit by unit, not all at once.
    modernize_years: 10,
    // Multiplies the forces' consumption in peace (not the wear of their equipment). 1: the
    // catalogue as written. The rest of the military budget is spent anyway (equipment beyond
    // the needs), so a larger scale only moves money from that to training ammunition, which
    // the 1925 arms industry does not make enough of (docs/war.pt-BR.md, "Forças em paz").
    upkeep_scale: "1",
    share_override: [],
    // Orders of forces (slice A2). Weeks over which the men of a land order report, a class at
    // a time; their training then takes the type's training weeks (design parameter).
    raise_weeks: 12,
    // Weeks from laying down to delivery, peacetime pace, for the types that join whole
    // (docs/war.pt-BR.md, "Encomenda de forças"); a type not listed takes 52.
    // The historical rule of the forces' composition (docs/war.pt-BR.md, "Composição das
    // forças"): once a year the infantry converts toward a share of the land men on wheels and on
    // tracks, the target of the newest generation of the infantry's equipment the country makes.
    // Design values; the calibration adjusts them, the structure stays.
    //   - Generation 0 (1925's): 150 on wheels and 50 on tracks, inside the range of the
    //     sources for 1939-44: about a seventh of the German divisions in 1939 and 52 of 322 in
    //     November 1943; the French 7 motorized, 3 light mechanized and 3 armoured divisions of
    //     some 90 in May 1940; the American 14 armoured and 5 motorized of 73 at the end of 1942.
    //   - Generation 1 (mass production of arms): halfway, 300 and 100.
    //   - Generation 2 (guided missiles, from about 1960): the large armies all on wheels or
    //     tracks (the US Army's ROAD divisions of 1961, the mechanized infantry among them).
    // At most 20 per mille of the land men a year (a class of equipment).
    composition: Some((
        targets: [(0, 150, 50), (1, 300, 100), (2, 650, 350)],
        per_year: 20,
        wheels_unit: "mechanized",
        tracks_unit: "armor",
        from_unit: "infantry",
        // A formation counts as moving on wheels or tracks by these goods it holds.
        vehicles: ["automobiles", "armored_vehicles"],
        // The rule opens no new conversion while the army holds less than 90 % of the
        // equipment it needs (design value): a country that cannot keep its infantry armed does
        // not motorize it.
        min_fill: 900,
    )),
    // The AI's orders of new ships and aircraft (`war::ai_orders`, item 11): each January a
    // country without a player turns `share` of the year's leftover over the needs (what went to
    // the depot) into orders, split between the sea and the air by one rule for everyone, each
    // service in the shape it has, within the treaty caps in force. DESIGN VALUES, without a
    // source: no page was found for the split of military spending by service.
    // `share` is 0 (the orchestrator, 2026-11-23): the military ceiling gives 5 to 7 % of GDP in
    // peace against 1 to 3 % in the 1930s, and at 500 the AI built fleets and air forces 50 to
    // 400 times the real ones (docs/war.pt-BR.md). It rises once the ceiling is recalibrated.
    ai_orders: Some((
        share: 0,
        sea_base: 300,
        sea_coast: 400,
        sea_colonies: 400,
        air_base: 500,
    )),
    // The reserve by cohort (`war::reserve`, item 9; switch `reserve_cohorts`, off).
    // - training_loss: training a cohort loses a year out of service, per mille. DESIGN VALUE,
    //   without a source (the 1920s laws had refresher courses: Czechoslovakia 14 weeks in all,
    //   Armaments Year-Book 1924, p. 346).
    // - reserve_years: years in the reserve where the country has no figure. DESIGN VALUE.
    // - law_months: months of service by the conscription effect where the country has no
    //   figure (volunteer 0, selective 12, universal 24). DESIGN VALUES.
    // - min_months, max_months: the bounds of the player's command.
    // - countries: (tag, months of service, years in the reserve; 0 = reserve_years), from the
    //   League of Nations' Armaments Year-Book, 1st ed., A.37.1924.IX (Geneva, 1924): France 18
    //   months and 28 years of reserve (p. 431); Italy 18 months (p. 520); Poland 24, reserve to
    //   40 (p. 647); Czechoslovakia 14, reserve to 40 (pp. 345-346); Romania 24, 18 years of
    //   reserve (p. 669); Soviet Union 24, reserve to 40 (p. 777); Japan 24, then 4 years 4
    //   months and 10 years of reserve (p. 566); Spain 24, 18 years in all (p. 691); Belgium
    //   12 (p. 45); Netherlands 5.5 months, to 40 (p. 590); Sweden 165 to 225 days (p. 718); Switzerland 65 days, liable from 20 to 48 (pp. 744, 748);
    //   Germany: conscription abolished, 12-year volunteers (p. 476); the United Kingdom and the
    //   United States: volunteers (pp. 83-100, 805). Years to 40 counted from leaving service
    //   at 21 plus the service (my computation).
    reserve_cohorts: Some((
        training_loss: 50,
        reserve_years: 15,
        law_months: [(0, 0), (300, 12), (800, 24)],
        min_months: 6,
        max_months: 36,
        countries: [
            ("FRA", 18, 28), ("ITA", 18, 0), ("POL", 24, 17), ("CZE", 14, 18),
            ("ROM", 24, 18), ("SOV", 24, 17), ("JAP", 24, 14), ("ESP", 24, 16),
            ("BEL", 12, 0), ("NLD", 6, 19), ("SWE", 7, 0), ("CHE", 2, 28), ("GER", 0, 0), ("GBR", 0, 0),
            ("USA", 0, 0),
        ],
    )),
    // The nuclear programme (docs/war.pt-BR.md, "Arma nuclear na escada de crise"; N1 and N2,
    // decided by the owner on 2026-11-19). The money buys a laboratory's goods until the bomb
    // can be built; each month what was delivered is credited to the first technology of the
    // chain still to understand or prototype, at `dollars_per_cost` (base prices) a point of its
    // cost. Calibrated relative to GDP: the Manhattan Project cost some 2 billion dollars of
    // 1945 over 1942-45 (Hewlett and Anderson, 1962, The New World), about 0.4 % of the American
    // GDP a year for three years, for some 1,800 points of the chain (enrichment and the bomb,
    // science and prototypes); with the game's American GDP of about 8e11 (1925) to 1.2e12,
    // that is some 8 million a point.
    nuclear_programme: Some((
        chain: ["nuclear_fission", "uranium_enrichment", "production_reactor", "atomic_bomb", "thermonuclear_weapon"],
        // N2: the weapons are copied at half the usual pace (sharing by a clause between allies
        // waits for the treaties' clause).
        weapons: ["atomic_bomb", "thermonuclear_weapon"],
        imitation: 500,
        basket: [
            ("electronic_components", 300), ("machinery", 300), ("chemicals", 200),
            ("electricity", 200),
        ],
        dollars_per_cost: 8000000,
        // The programme is the state buyer of its prototypes, at the most a buyer can be.
        buyer: 1500,
        // Three quarters of the money keep the warhead order going once the bomb can be built.
        stock_share: 750,
        // At most 2 % of GDP a year (the Manhattan Project was about 0.4 %).
        max_share: 20,
        plant: "weapon_core_plant",
        plant_shortage: 900,
        plant_weeks: 26,
        // U1 (the owner, 2026-11-19): the programme opens uranium mines with the state's capital
        // in deposits already known, at home and then in a colony, when the market delivers less
        // than nine tenths of the ore its core plants ask for; one request at a time, as the
        // plants.
        mine: Some("uranium_mine"),
        mine_shortage: 900,
        // The assembly of the warhead: its plant once the cores run, and another level when the
        // market delivers less than nine tenths of the assemblies the warhead order asks for.
        assembly: Some("weapon_assembly_plant"),
        assembly_shortage: 900,
        // The warhead the programme orders. The order grows with the money for the stock (no
        // batch): the money and what the plants deliver are its only ceilings.
        warhead: "nuclear_warhead",
    )),
    build_weeks: [
        ("battleship", 156), ("carrier", 156), ("cruiser", 104), ("destroyer", 52),
        ("submarine", 65), ("escort", 39), ("coastal", 39), ("mine_warfare", 26),
        ("transport", 52), ("missile_submarine", 260),
        ("fighter", 26), ("attack", 26), ("strategic_bomber", 52), ("patrol", 26),
        ("air_transport", 26), ("naval_aviation", 26), ("helicopter", 26), ("drone", 26),
        ("ballistic_missile", 104), ("nuclear_warhead", 52), ("satellite", 156),
        ("missile_defense", 208),
    ],
)
